===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|military|also|attributively|and|figuratively}}
## An [[attack#Noun|attack]] [[make#Verb|made]] by [[troop#Noun|troops]] from a [[besieged#Adjective|besieged]] [[position#Noun|position]]; a [[sally#Etymology 2|sally]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Thomas Jones|John T[homas] Jones]]|chapter=[Notes.] Note 35 [Observations on the Several Sorties Made by the Garrisons of the Places Besieged in Spain].|title=Journals of Sieges Carried on by the Army under the [[w:Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of Wellington|Duke of Wellington]], in Spain, between the Years 1811 and 1814.{{nb...|Second Edition. In Two Volumes.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed [by]}} [C. Roworth] for [[w:Thomas Egerton (publisher)|T[homas] Egerton]],{{nb...|bookseller to the Ordnance, Military Library, Whitehall.}}|year=1827|volume=II|page=369|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/journalsofsiege02jone/page/369/mode/1up|oclc=1249842094|passage=The events of these sieges show that a bold and vigorous '''sortie''' in force might carry destruction through every part of a besieger's approaches, where the guard is injudiciously disposed and ill commanded; but that if due precautions have been observed in forming the approaches and posting the defenders, any '''sortie''' from a besieged place must be checked with loss in their advance, when the approaches are still distant; or when the approaches are near, should a '''sortie''' succeed in pushing into them by a sudden rush, the assailants must inevitably be driven out again in a moment, with terrible slaughter.}}
## {{lb|en|aviation}} An [[operational]] [[flight#Noun|flight]] [[carry out|carried out]] by a [[single#Adjective|single]] [[military#Adjective|military]] [[aircraft]].
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Aron Heller|title=These Jewish World War II Veterans Would Be Legends, if People Knew Their Stories|work=The New York Times|date=2019-05-08|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/08/magazine/canadian-jewish-veterans-world-war-ii.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Their aircraft had no belly gunners and were at the mercy of Luftwaffe fighters that attacked from below. Whenever they lifted off on a mission, they departed with the knowledge that this '''sortie''' could easily be their last.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Maria Varenikova; Andrew E. Kramer|title=How Ukraine’s Outgunned Air Force Is Fighting Back Against Russian Jets|work=The New York Times|date=2022-03-22|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/03/22/world/europe/ukraine-air-force-russia.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=They are vastly outnumbered: Russia is believed to fly some 200 '''sorties''' per day while Ukraine flies five to 10.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}}
## An [[act#Noun|act]] of [[venture#Verb|venturing]] out to [[do#Verb|do]] a [[task#Noun|task]], etc.
##* {{quote-web|en|url=http://www.theguardian.com/uk/2007/apr/15/britishidentity.fiction|title=Absolute MacInnes|author=Ed Vulliamy|date=2007-04-14|work=The Guardian|passage=‘I'm just not interested in the whole class crap that seems to needle you and all the tax-payers,’ the teenager tells some ‘pre-historic monster’ of an adult, with a ‘cool’ snobbishness which MacInnes's companion on many of his Notting Hill '''sorties''', the late Professor Richard Wollheim, compared to the ‘Sang Froid’ of Baudelaire's Dandy as he cruised through Fin-de-Siecle Paris with a similar sensibility, or lack of it.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Jeff Foust|title=NASA’s Lunar Space Station Is a Great/Terrible Idea|journal=w:IEEE Spectrum|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20211128090738/https://spectrum.ieee.org/nasas-lunar-space-station-is-a-greatterrible-idea|archivedate=28 November 2021|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers|date=8 July 2019|issn=0018-9235|oclc=888477014|passage=Finally, the astronauts will descend to the lunar surface. After their '''sortie''' on the moon, they'll return to the orbital station.}}
## {{lb|en|figuratively}}
### An act of [[try#Verb|trying]] to [[enter#Verb|enter]] a [[new#Adjective|new]] [[field#Noun|field]] of [[activity]].
### {{lb|en|sports}} An [[attacking#Adjective|attacking]] [[move#Noun|move]].
###* {{quote-book|en|author=David Potter; Tom Campbell|title=Jock Stein: The Celtic Years|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=1GBb9phYrywC&pg=RA1-PT27|location=Edinburgh|publisher=w:Mainstream Publishing|year=1998|year_published=2005|isbn=978-1-84018-241-5|passage={{w|Kai Johansen}} made a '''sortie''' down the right and, running out of ideas, tried a shot from more than 20 yards.}}
## {{lb|en|astronautics}} An operational flight carried out by a [[spacecraft]] [[involve|involving]] a [[return#Noun|return]] to [[Earth]].
## {{lb|en|military}} {{synonym of|en|sally port|t=an [[entry]] to or [[opening#Noun|opening]] into a [[fortification]] to [[enable]] a [[sally#Noun|sally]]}}
##* {{RQ:Bulwer-Lytton Harold|volume=III|chapter=VII|pages=331–332|pageref=332|passage=[I]t was all encompassed by the palisades and breastworks, to which were but three '''sorties''', whence the defenders might sally, or through which at need the vanguard might secure a retreat.}}
## {{lb|en|photography}} A [[series]] of [[aerial#Adjective|aerial]] [[photograph#Noun|photographs]] [[take#Verb|taken]] during the flight of an aircraft; {{lb|en|by extension}} a [[photography]] [[session#Noun|session]].

